has the batman found a fresh approach to  some very familiar material or is it  flying around in the dark keep watching  to find out what the critics are saying  about this highly anticipated release  the various tv and movie adaptations of  batman over the past 80 years or so have  run the gamut from colorful and campy to  grim and violent the latest installment  of the franchise is director matt reeves  the batman in which robert pattinson  takes the reins as billionaire turned  vigilante bruce wayne it reportedly  follows him throughout his second year  of fighting crime in gotham city while  protecting the city he faces off against  paul danos riddler a serial killer who  begins targeting high-profile residents  and broadcasting his killing spree this  will be the notorious villain's first  appearance in a batman film since 1995's  batman forever in which jim carrey's  portrayal of the puzzle-obsessed  criminal earned critical praise after  facing production delays because of the  coven 19 pandemic the film is finally  slated for its theatrical release on  march 4th the batman also stars zoe  kravitz as catwoman jeffrey wright as  police lieutenant james gordon andy  circus as alfred pennyworth and colin  farrell as the penguin the first wave of  critical reaction to the film was  positive with many praising the gritty  return to form but as the release date  draws closer more nuanced takes are  coming out as of the making of this  video the batman has a 91 approval  rating on rotten tomatoes so it's  largely faring well with critics in a  review for strange harbors jeffrey zhang  praised the film's fresh direction given  just how much other superhero fare is  out there zhang wrote the batman is a  grand mosaic of interlocking pieces that  fit together as a refreshing antidote to  superhero fatigue he singled out kravitz  performance as the highlight  you got a lot of cats everything about  strength similarly in her review for  insider kirsten akuna said that there  isn't a weak link in the cast she also  dismissed concerns about the film's  nearly three hour run time and took a  big swing stating that robert pattinson  is the best live-action batman since  michael keaton now you want to get nuts  come on let's get nuts she added that  reeves and the crew quote had reverence  for batman's history on and off the  screen ign's alex stedman called the  batman a gripping gorgeous and at times  genuinely scary film and praised its  psychologically dark portrayal of bruce  wayne like the other critics she noted  how this film is totally unlike its  predecessors while still capturing the  heart of the source material for slash  film chris evangelista argued that this  might be the best batman movie to date  writing i know there will be plenty of  people who feel they are burned out on  all things batman that there couldn't  possibly be room for yet another  retelling of the same old tale but the  batman defies all the odds although the  batman has received plenty of praise  some critics were less than enthralled  by the long runtime and dark color  palette in his review for the san  francisco chronicle mick lasalle  concluded that as a whole the batman is  mostly just a collection of bad ideas he  praised zoe kravitz and paul dano's  performances but expressed confusion  about robert pattinson's performance  calling it stiff justice  the answer's justice lasalle also  questioned reeve's dystopian direction  calling it very 2019 as pre-pandemic as  skin-tight jeans he added reeves comes  up with a knock-off of joker only  without the wit and without the great  performance just a flimsy idea that  everything is awful corrupt and rotten  and he assumes that his concept is so  deep and profound that he needs three  hours to develop it for paste magazine  jacob allar called the film ambitious  and dedicated to its vision but said it  can't crack how to make the world's  greatest detective seem like one at all  aller says pattinson's batman is not  different enough to outrun his legacy's  ever-growing shadow polygon's joshua  rivera wrote that the film's biggest  issue is its inability to stand out  among the rest of the franchise he wrote  the batman is frustratingly safe a movie  full of potential for more and settling  for less it preaches to the choir  reinforcing the same ideas trodden over  and over again across five movies  multiple video games and every comic  book in the mold of frank miller and  david matsukelli's batman year one you  can decide for yourself when the batman  hits theaters march 4th 2022. check out  one of our newest videos right here plus  even more looper videos about the batman  are coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

